    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d730">

                <pb facs="0948" n="849" xml:id="pg849"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.001 Somoza, Anastasio/2: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-12-19T13:00:00-05:00">December 19, 1936—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>137. Your 240, December 14, 5 p.m.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d730-f1"/> You should of
                    course attend the inaugural ceremonies. Keep the Department informed by
                    telegraph if additional special ambassadors are named.</p>
                <p>Your 241, December 15, 10 a.m. It is not customary for the President to send
                    congratulatory telegrams to Presidents-elect either at time of election or
                    inauguration, and it is not believed that an exception should be made in this
                    case.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Moore</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
